#!spectest
# mode: run
# adjudicator: ggdef
# since: spec-v0.1
# features: [move, liveness]
# doc: |
#   Static double-move of a ConsumeCallable (verdict = elaborate ∘ eval). A
#   `ConsumeCallable[..]` value is single-owner: calling it CONSUMES it, so the
#   second call `f(10)` is a double move of an already-consumed slot. The
#   flow-sensitive liveness gate rejects the second call BEFORE eval with
#   E_DoubleMove — stdout stays EXACTLY empty (the program never ran, which IS the
#   verdict), exit 1. All four lanes REJECT: ggdef's consume-call kill fires on the
#   first call, so the second reads a moved-out slot. Sibling of reject_double_move.gg
#   on the ConsumeCallable axis; flipping the expected `reject:` code must make every
#   lane MISMATCH (Core #6/#8).
# expect:
#   exit: 1
#   stdout: ""
#   reject: E_DoubleMove
#!end

void main():
    ConsumeCallable[int(int)] f = !(n): n * 2
    int r1 = f(5)
    int r2 = f(10)
    print(f"{r1}{r2}")
